WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
The number of blue crabs in the Chesapeake Bay can change by more than 100 million

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.100
from one winter to the next.

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.240
But no population can keep growing forever.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.980
Food and hiding places are limited.

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.460
Predators, disease, temperature and salinity affect survival too.

6
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.420
Together, resources and challenges set the largest population an environment can

7
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.460
support over time.

8
00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.740
That is its carrying capacity.

9
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.213
A population is one species in one place at one time.

10
00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:37.353
A limiting factor keeps it from growing larger.

11
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.353
Biotic factors are living parts of the environment, like predators and disease.

12
00:00:43.473 --> 00:00:48.313
Abiotic factors are nonliving parts, like temperature and salinity, the salt level.

13
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:51.393
Crowding makes competition and disease worse.

14
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:55.653
Suppose 10 bacteria land in a dish of fresh food.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.813
Each one splits in two every 30 minutes.

16
00:00:59.713 --> 00:01:03.833
Now there are 20, then 40, 80, 160, and 320.

17
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:06.413
This is exponential growth.

18
00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:09.653
It makes a J shape while resources are plentiful.

19
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.107
Then limits set in.

20
00:01:12.907 --> 00:01:18.687
Food runs short. In the simplest model, growth slows into an S shape and levels off

21
00:01:18.687 --> 00:01:19.827
near carrying capacity.

22
00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:24.907
If few individuals move in or out, births and deaths are about equal there.

23
00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:29.447
Small ups and downs around that level are called dynamic equilibrium.

24
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:35.670
Carrying capacity can change when food, space or shelter change and stay changed.

25
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.270
More hiding places can raise it.

26
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.750
Losing marsh grass can lower it.

27
00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.010
A single hard freeze is different.

28
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:49.250
If resources stay the same, it pushes the population below carrying capacity, and

29
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.750
the population can grow back.

30
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.410
Crabbing also removes individuals.

31
00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.010
Fewer crabs does not by itself mean a lower carrying capacity.

32
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:00.553
What does the winter survey show?

33
00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:05.893
In the last two survey winters, the total rose mostly because there were more young

34
00:02:05.893 --> 00:02:08.213
crabs. Adult females fell.

35
00:02:09.113 --> 00:02:10.613
These are population estimates.

36
00:02:11.413 --> 00:02:15.853
The survey does not measure carrying capacity or tell us which factor caused the

37
00:02:15.853 --> 00:02:20.413
change. We need temperature, habitat and predator data to test that.

38
00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:25.373
The central rule is that environmental limits constrain population growth.
